81248507648706,0.0,0.0,0.0,-1.680836794548668e-05,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.122638039290905,0.0,-0.0006513857515528798,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.009672796353697777,-6.05564855504781e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.3439105749130249,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-4.851700214203447e-05,-4.887569048150908e-06,-2.264974000354414e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,97794,400,25411,3,323,400,1867,35,3,525,220,374,436,346,642,448,400,1867,28,1706,28,9548,54876,323,57960,1975,1056,90,9548,31716,88184,57960,1975,1056,90,1706,31716,518,400,36,12947,1416,57960,1975,1056,90,9548,11035,712,79,59,1975,1056,90,1706,31716,11,1221,1128,374,279,897,315,57960,4044,356,41715,4044,422,3,30,508,6405,921,474,297,13842,62944,26,1159,17047,26,1379,7,16,20,15,1215,1638,2752,2333,258,28357,7,15,13,23,1106,7633,45375,313,7,3741,7,22,15,593,313,7,17,9,54224,7,22,15,701,15,29621,21621,317,7633,45375,313,7,3741,7,17,15,593,313,7,3741,7,22,15,593,313,21621,317,12670,468,284,19246,2768,45375,313,3741,7,17,15,701,3741,7,22,15,29621,7,17,9,54224,7,22,15,701,15,1106,16119,20912,36,73315,36,317,12670,362,284,5419,7,22,15,317,12670,425,284,6238,280,12670,356,284,320,17,9,54224,7,22,15,701,15,317,12670,422,284,5419,7,17,15,317,16119,20912,32,73315,32,22037,317,16119,20912,33,73315,33,11,16844,317,16119,20912,34,73315,34,11,925,317,16119,20912,35,73315,35,42125,317,7633,27704,524,3433,838,5349,42125,15031,11,16,13,20,1106,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nTriangles $ABC$ and $ABD$ are  isosceles with $AB=AC=BD$, and $\\overline{BD}$ intersects $\\overline{AC}$ at $E$. If $\\overline{BD}\\perp\\overline{AC}$, then what is the value of $\\angle C+\\angle D$? [asy]\nimport olympiad; import geometry; size(150); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));\ndraw(origin--(dir(70))--(2*Cos(70),0)--cycle);\ndraw(origin--(dir(20))--(dir(70))--cycle);\npair E = intersectionpoint(origin--dir(20),dir(70)--(2*Cos(70),0));\ndot(\"$E$\",E);\npair A = dir(70);\npair B = origin;\npair C = (2*Cos(70),0);\npair D = dir(20);\ndot(\"$A$\",A,N);\ndot(\"$B$\",B,SW);\ndot(\"$C$\",C,SE);\ndot(\"$D$\",D,E);\ndraw(rightanglemark(B,E,A,1.5));\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"988688e60c0300f10e77cda07e69490535993fb5a8a71e8198944cd7addf287d"},"seconds":24.102924346923828,"seed":700067704967390321,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by analyzing the given geometric configuration. We have two isosceles triangles \\( \\triangle ABC \\) and \\( \\triangle ABD \\) with \\( AB = AC = BD \\), and \\( \\overline{BD} \\perp \\overline{AC} \\). The intersection point of \\( \\overline{BD} \\) and \\( \\overline{AC} \\) is \\( E \\).\n\nFirst, let's determine the angles in \\( \\triangle ABD \\). Since \\( BD \\perp AC \\), \\( \\angle BEC = 90^\\circ \\). Because \\( \\triangle ABD \\) is isosceles with \\( AB = BD \\), the base angles \\( \\angle BAD \\) and \\( \\angle ABD \\) are equal. Let \\( \\angle BAD = \\angle ABD = x \\). Then, the vertex angle \\( \\angle ADB \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ - 2x\n\\]\n\nNext, we consider \\( \\triangle ABC \\). Since \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is isosceles with \\( AB = AC \\), the base angles \\( \\angle ABC \\) and \\( \\angle ACB \\) are equal. Let \\( \\angle ABC = \\angle ACB = y \\). Then, the vertex angle \\( \\angle BAC \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\angle BAC = 180^\\circ - 2y\n\\]\n\nSince \\( E \\) is the intersection of \\( BD \\) and \\( AC \\), we can use the fact that \\( \\angle AEB = 90^\\circ \\) (as \\( BD \\perp AC \\)). This implies that \\( \\angle AED = 90^\\circ \\).\n\nNow, consider the straight line \\( \\overline{AC} \\). The sum of the angles around point \\( E \\) on \\( \\overline{AC} \\) is \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\angle AEB + \\angle BEC + \\angle CED = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nSubstituting the known angles:\n\\[\n90^\\circ + 90^\\circ + \\angle CED = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n180^\\circ + \\angle CED = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle CED = 0^\\circ\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction, so we need to re-evaluate our approach. Instead, we focus on the angles in the triangles directly.\n\nSince \\( \\angle AEB = 90^\\circ \\), we can use the fact that \\( \\angle AED = 90^\\circ \\). This implies that \\( \\angle AED = 90^\\circ \\).\n\nNow, consider the angles in \\( \\triangle ABD \\):\n\\[\n\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ - 2x\n\\]\nSince \\( \\angle ADB \\) is also part of \\( \\triangle ABD \\) and \\( \\angle ABD = x \\), we have:\n\\[\n180^\\circ - 2x = 90^\\circ\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n2x = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, consider the angles in \\( \\triangle ABC \\):\n\\[\n\\angle BAC = 180^\\circ - 2y\n\\]\nSince \\( \\angle BAC \\) is also part of \\( \\triangle ABC \\) and \\( \\angle ABC = y \\), we have:\n\\[\n180^\\circ - 2y = 90^\\circ\n\\]\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n2y = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\( \\angle C + \\angle D \\):\n\\[\n\\angle C = y = 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle D = 180^\\circ - 2x = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle C + \\angle D = 45^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 135^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( \\angle C + \\angle D \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{135^\\circ}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{135^\\circ}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:77"}
